  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe latest surface weather map shows a cold front stretching from \nsouthwestern SD to northeastern SD. Relative humidity values will be \nin the 30 to 40 percent range (dewpoints in the 40s) north of the \nfront, while in the 60-85% range south (dewpoints in the 60s). Off \nand on showers will continue over mainly central SD near the \nboundary. A few have been strong to severe from mid morning through \nthe early afternoon hours. While the intensity of these storms has \ndiminished, a few showers linger linger across central SD.\n\nThe cold front will push north tonight, into ND by daybreak Monday \nas low pressure surges north across WY. Winds will increase out of \nthe south during the day Monday ahead of the surface low that will \nset up across western SD. Gusts of 15-30kts are expected Monday \nafternoon, strongest west of the James River. More steady rain west \nof the Missouri River early Monday morning will expand across \nnortheastern SD late Monday into Tuesday, before slowly ending west \nto east during the day Tuesday as the surface low crosses central to \nnortheastern SD. \n\nWeak thunderstorms will accompany some of the rain, with the CAMs \nindicating the highest potential in the near term being over north \ncentral SD 10-14Z Monday. Forecast rainfall amounts have diminished \naround 0.25 in a few locations, mainly over northern SD. The current \nforecast range is from around a tenth of an inch to potentially up \ntoo an inch. The highest rainfall amounts are forecast over north \ncentral SD.\n\nA large area of high pressure over MT Thursday morning will push \nacross the Dakotas Thursday and over MN southeastern SD on Friday. \nThis will bring cooler and drier weather to the forecast area, with \nhighs mainly in the 60s Thursday through Sunday.",
